<section>
    <section>
        <h2>The Developer Gap</h2>
        <p>Despite growing adoption of linked open data, ontology engineers and software teams speak different languages.</p>
        <p>OWL/RDF-based data models are often <strong>too abstract</strong> for developers to implement against directly.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h3>The challenge</h3>
        <p>Documentation and traceability help—but keeping them aligned with a <strong>continuously evolving ontology and codebase</strong> is unsustainable manually.</p>
    </section>
</section>

<section>
    <section>
        <h2>ODDToolkit</h2>
        <p>An extensible open-source toolkit that generates implementation artefacts from an OWL ontology enriched with a SKOS concept scheme.</p>
        <div class="mermaid">
            flowchart LR
                OWL[("OWL Ontology")]
                SKOS[("SKOS Concept Scheme")]
                CONFIG["⚙️ Config Rules"]
                PIPELINE("ODDToolkit Pipeline")
                ARTEFACTS["📦 Artefacts"]

                OWL --> PIPELINE
                SKOS --> PIPELINE
                CONFIG --> PIPELINE
                PIPELINE --> ARTEFACTS
        </div>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h3>SKOS naming bridge</h3>
        <p>A SKOS concept scheme acts as an <strong>explicit naming bridge</strong>: ontology predicates are projected to developer-friendly identifiers without modifying the ontology itself.</p>
    </section>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Generated Artefacts</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Visual UML class and ER diagrams</li>
        <li>SQL schemas</li>
        <li>Java and TypeScript models</li>
        <li>SHACL shapes</li>
        <li>JSON-LD frames</li>
        <li>Bikeshed specification documents</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Real-World Use Case</h2>
    <p>Demonstrated using a <strong>real-world industrial emissions ontology</strong> for environmental reporting (Flemish Government).</p>
    <p>ODDToolkit generated documentation and implementation artefacts for development teams, integrated into CI workflows.</p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Conclusion &amp; Future Work</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>ODDToolkit closes the gap between ontology engineers and developers</li>
        <li>CI integration keeps artefacts synchronised with the ontology</li>
        <li>Future: additional generators, richer SKOS mapping rules, community plugins</li>
    </ul>
    <p><small>Source code available · SEMANTICS 2026</small></p>
</section>
